1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:27,399
HOST: In August 1680, in the deserts of New Mexico, the Spanish Empire lost a war. Lost it to people it had already beaten. The Pueblo peoples rose up, killed hundreds of colonists, burned the churches, and drove the Spanish out completely.  It is the only Native revolt in North American history that threw the colonizers out entirely. And for twelve years, the Pueblos held their country.

2
00:00:27,399 --> 00:00:43,600
HOST: By the end of this lesson: One: the causes, mission labor, drought, and religious repression. Two: how Popé organized the revolt village by village. Three: what the revolt's success proves about Indigenous agency and the limits of empire.

3
00:00:43,600 --> 00:01:08,200
HOST: To understand 1680, start eighty years earlier. In 1598, the conquistador Juan de Oñate occupied the mesa fortress of Acoma Pueblo. When the Acoma refused to hand over winter supplies, they killed fifteen Spaniards, including Oñate's nephew.  Oñate answered with cannons. More than eight hundred Acoma died. The man who ordered it was Oñate himself, governor of New Mexico.

4
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:22,200
ONATE: The survivors will stand trial. Every man over twenty-five will lose one foot. The rest will serve the Spanish as slaves.

5
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:49,599
HOST: The terror worked, for a while. For eighty years the Pueblos lived under Spanish rule, and the pressure built.  The mission system demanded labor in the fields while priests punished Pueblo ceremonies as witchcraft. Drought starved the villages in the 1670s. Then in 1675, the governor arrested forty-seven Pueblo religious leaders. Three were hanged. The rest were whipped.  Among the survivors was a man named Popé.

6
00:01:49,599 --> 00:02:20,199
HOST: Popé fled north to Taos Pueblo, beyond the Spanish reach. And there he did what no one had done before: he united the villages.  A cord with knots tied along its length. What was it for?  It is a calendar, and a battle plan. Runners carried knotted cords from village to village, and each morning the leaders untied one knot. When the last knot came undone, every village would rise on the same day.

7
00:02:20,199 --> 00:02:34,199
POPE: Drive out the Spanish. Burn their churches. Never speak the names of their gods again. Return to the old ways, and the old gods will return to us.

8
00:02:34,199 --> 00:02:56,199
HOST: On August 10, 1680, the knots ran out. Across New Mexico, Pueblo warriors attacked at once. They killed more than three hundred Spaniards, including twenty-one of the thirty-three Franciscan priests. They burned the churches and besieged Santa Fe. The survivors fled south to El Paso.  New Mexico was Pueblo country again.

9
00:02:56,199 --> 00:03:32,000
HOST: For twelve years, the Pueblos governed themselves. They tore down the missions and danced the old dances in the open.  When the Spanish returned in 1692, they came back changed. They appointed a defender for Pueblo rights, granted Pueblo families land, and stopped trying to crush the old religion.  The revolt had worked. It proved that Indigenous people could defeat a European empire, and it forced that empire to rule differently.  Pressure, plus a network, plus timing: that is how a scattered resistance becomes a coordinated one.

10
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:42,000
